e('| Deadlock | Blocked | No (in the cycle) |','| Deadlock | Unable to proceed; may sleep or spin | No (among affected participants) |','Spinning deadlock can consume CPU.')
e('| Livelock | Running | No |','| Livelock | Repeated state changes/retries | No useful progress by affected participants |','May include sleeps and partialsystem progress.')
e("No thread is ever *blocked*, so there's no cycle in any wait-for graph, and deadlock detection sees nothing wrong. CPU sits at 100%.","In this particular repeating schedule neither thread blocks indefinitely, so a blocked-wait cycle detector misses the failure. CPU use can be high. Other schedules may briefly block at `first.acquire()`. The caller must release both locks after a successful return.",'Actualacquireblocking;nofixedCPUload.')
e('In practice, scheduler noise usually knocks the threads out of step eventually, which is what makes livelock nasty: it shows up as unpredictable bursts of wasted CPU and latency rather than a clean hang.','Scheduling variation may break this pattern, but it supplies no progress guarantee; repeated collisions can waste CPU and increase latency.','Unsupported frequency removed.')
e('Livelock needs the participants to behave identically. Make them behave differently:','For this symmetric retry pattern, useful ways to improve progress include:','General livelock doesnotrequireidentical behavior.')
e("Linux's NAPI fixes this by switching from interrupts to polling under load (module 7).","Linux's NAPI uses interrupt-triggered, budgeted polling to mitigate receive livelock (module 7); it does not guarantee application progress under every overload or configuration.",'Pollingnotuniversalfix.',['https://docs.kernel.org/networking/napi.html'])
e('Back-off or wound-wait style timestamps fix it.','Back-off can reduce collisions; timestamp protocols need fair execution and finite transactions for eventual completion.','Retryjitter notformalguarantee.')
e('Linux used ticket spinlocks from 2.6.25 for exactly this reason, before moving to queued (MCS-style) spinlocks, which are also FIFO but scale better.','This teaching lock assumes each successful acquirer eventually releases exactly once, no cancelled ticket, and fair scheduling. It has no ownership checks or timeout/cancellation support. Kernel queued spinlocks use more complex architecture-dependent fast paths and queueing; they should not be described as this exact FIFO algorithm.','Unqualified kernelFIFO andproductioncapabilities removed.',lk)
e('Fairness costs throughput. If the lock must go to a waiter that\'s asleep, the CPU idles while that thread is woken and scheduled, which takes microseconds, while the releasing thread could have re-acquired it in nanoseconds. Under heavy contention this creates a **lock convoy**: every hand-off pays a context switch.','Strict hand-off can reduce throughput: the lock may sit unused until the designated waiter runs, even though another thread could acquire it. The CPU can run unrelated work. Repeated hand-off delays can create a **lock convoy**, but costs depend on scheduling and workload.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> Fixed nanosecond/microsecond hand-off costs are omitted because no reproducible benchmark record is provided.','Remove unsupported timings andCPUidleassertion.',ja)
e('`new ReentrantLock(true)` gives FIFO ordering at a throughput cost.','`new ReentrantLock(true)` favors the longest-waiting thread under contention; untimed `tryLock()` can still barge, and lock fairness does not guarantee scheduler fairness.', 'JavaAPIexplicitcaveats.',ja)
e("Go's design is a good default: be unfair while it's cheap (high throughput) and switch to fair when someone has waited too long (bounded tail latency).","Go's policy illustrates a throughput/fairness compromise. Its 1 ms threshold is an implementation detail, not a maximum lock-wait or tail-latency guarantee.",'Waitdependscriticalsection scheduling.',go)
e('Concurrent algorithms are often classified by the liveness they guarantee, from strongest to weakest:','Concurrent algorithms use the following progress properties. The first three form an implication hierarchy; lock guarantees additionally assume holders release and threads are scheduled:','Do not totalorder incomparable lockproperties.')
e('| Wait-free | Every thread finishes in bounded steps |','| Wait-free | Each operation completes within a bounded number of its own steps |','Notwallclockpausedthreads.')
e('| Lock-free | Some thread always finishes |','| Lock-free | Continued collective steps guarantee system-wide operation progress |','No guarantee pausedthreadscompletion.')
e('| Obstruction-free | A thread running alone finishes |','| Obstruction-free | An operation completes after sufficiently many steps in isolation |','Finiteisolation requirement.')
e('The first three are **non-blocking**: a thread that is paused (descheduled, page-faulting) can\'t stop the others. The last two describe locks: a fair lock is starvation-free; a plain unfair mutex is only deadlock-free.','The first three do not require a paused participant to release a lock. Obstruction freedom is weaker than lock freedom: concurrent active operations may repeatedly interfere without progress. The lock guarantees assume finite critical sections and appropriate scheduling; nested dependencies can still deadlock even fair locks.','Obstructionfree notcollectiveprogress.')
e("A compare-and-swap retry loop is lock-free but not wait-free: some thread's CAS always succeeds, but one unlucky thread can lose every race and, in principle, starve.",'A correctly constructed atomic-counter CAS loop using lock-free primitives and bounded local work is lock-free, but not necessarily wait-free. A failed strong CAS indicates an intervening modification; a particular operation can keep losing. Arbitrary CAS loops and emulated atomics do not automatically have this guarantee.','CASitselfnotalgorithmguarantee.')
e('On Pathfinder, a high-priority bus management task waited on a mutex held by a low-priority meteorological task while medium-priority communications work ran; a watchdog noticed the bus task missing its deadline and reset the spacecraft. The VxWorks mutex had priority inheritance available but switched off.','Pathfinder engineer Glenn Reeves described an ASI/MET task holding a VxWorks pipe/select semaphore needed by `bc_dist`. Intermediate-priority work delayed ASI/MET; the higher-priority `bc_sched` detected that `bc_dist` had not completed its work and triggered a reset. Enabling priority inheritance for the relevant semaphore class fixed this failure.','UseprimaryengineeraccountconsistentOS02.',mars)
e("M can no longer preempt L, so H waits only for L's critical section.","M can no longer preempt L in this simple three-task model. A real bound must also account for higher-priority work, nested dependencies, I/O and scheduling assumptions.",'PIalone notcriticalsectiononlybound.',pi)
e("so no thread that might want the mutex can preempt it while it's held.","so lower-priority unrelated tasks cannot preempt it in the usual single-CPU fixed-priority model. Equal-priority scheduling and multiprocessor behavior need separate analysis.",'Immediateceilingassumptions.',pi)
e('That rule **prevents deadlock** and limits a high-priority thread to being blocked by at most one lower-priority critical section.','Under the protocol\'s single-processor fixed-priority model, correctly assigned ceilings, bounded critical sections and resource-use assumptions, that rule **prevents deadlock** and limits a job\'s lower-priority blocking to at most one critical section.','Theoremrequiresmodelassumptions.')
e("That's why real-time and UI code is told to wait on mutexes, not semaphores, across priority levels.","Choose a dependency protocol that exposes ownership where blocking is unavoidable; hard real-time audio code should generally avoid blocking on lower-priority work altogether.",'Mutexsubstitutiondoesnotmakeaudio deadline-safe.')
e('- Fair locks bound waiting but cost throughput (lock convoys); Go\'s starvation mode and Linux\'s hand-off flag switch to fairness only when needed.','- Fair ordering limits overtaking under scheduling/release assumptions, not wall-clock wait time. Hand-off can cost throughput; Go and Linux use adaptive policies.','Unboundedcriticalsection/schedulingstillpossible.',go)
e('- Priority inheritance bounds priority inversion for owned locks; priority ceilings also prevent deadlock but need static analysis; neither helps with semaphores or condition variables.','- Priority inheritance mitigates inversion on supported owned locks. Ceiling-protocol bounds need a suitable scheduling model and static analysis. Ownerless waits do not identify a producer to boost, though a condition wait\'s associated PI mutex may help during mutex reacquisition.','CVreacquirePI distinction.',pi)
f(1,'explanation','Deadlock means no progress through a dependency cycle; it can sleep or spin. Repeated successful acquisition/release and changing state here suggest livelock instead.','CPUalone insufficient.',th,o=0)
f(1,'explanation','A data race can also cause hangs or undefined behavior. The specific repeated acquire/release pattern makes livelock the best answer here.','Racescanpreventprogress.',th,o=3)
f(1,'workedExample','High CPU alone does not distinguish livelock from a spinning deadlock or other bug. Here the threads repeatedly acquire/release and change state without completing work, which points to livelock. Compare multiple stacks and progress counters, then inspect the retry protocol.','RemovecategoricalCPUheuristic.')
f(3,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[2]['prompt'].replace('prevent livelock','reduce the risk of repeated lockstep collisions'),'Jitternotproof.',aws)
f(3,'workedExample','Without delay, an interleaving can repeatedly make both threads fail and release. Random delays make that pattern less likely, but do not establish a deterministic bound or fix arbitrary livelocks. Successful callers must release both locks. A consistent order removes this particular two-lock cycle under the usual finite-critical-section and scheduling assumptions.','No unconditional symmetry/progressguarantee.',aws)
f(5,'explanation','Writer preference still has to wait for existing readers to release; it cannot give an exclusive writer ownership immediately. Reader preference can keep admitting new readers ahead of the writer.','Evenwriterpreferrednotimmediate.',th,o=3)
f(5,'explanation','Correct in the stated idealized arrival/holding schedule. Blocking new readers lets existing readers drain; scheduler fairness and finite holding times are still required.','Boundconditional.',th,o=2)
f(6,'prompt','Assume lock-free atomic primitives, a correctly implemented strong-CAS counter loop with bounded local work, and the usual scheduling/release assumptions for locks. Which statements about progress guarantees are true? Select all that apply.','Atomicprimitive may beblocking, arbitraryCASloop notlockfree.')
f(6,'workedExample','Wait-free guarantees bounded own-step completion for each operation. Lock-free guarantees collective progress, allowing individual starvation. Obstruction-free guarantees completion with sufficient isolation, but interference can stop collective progress. A strong-CAS counter loop under the stated assumptions is lock-free, not necessarily wait-free. A spinlock depends on its holder running and releasing. Fairness and eventual release are separate assumptions for starvation-free locks.','Clarifyownsteps/obstructionfree.')
f(8,'workedExample','Go\'s current implementation permits barging in normal mode. A waiter that wakes and has failed for over 1 ms can switch a still-locked mutex into starvation mode; this is not a timer that guarantees service at 1 ms. Starvation mode hands ownership to the queue head, then returns to normal when the recipient is last or its wait was short. This mitigates pathological tail latency but does not bound scheduler delays or critical-section length.','Noformalwallclockbound.',go)
f(9,'workedExample','A PI mutex identifies a current holder to boost. A semaphore does not identify which producer will post it, so enabling PI on unrelated mutexes does not accelerate L. Ensure producer scheduling and resource budgets meet deadlines, or decouple audio rendering with adequately buffered predecoded data and a suitable nonblocking handoff. Merely wrapping a queue in a PI mutex does not boost a producer that is not holding that mutex; buffers also require an underflow policy. A condition variable has no unique signaler, though reacquiring its associated PI mutex can invoke inheritance.','Originalmutexwrappingfixfalse;bufferunderflowlimits.',pi)
f(10,'text','Fair hand-off to a sleeping waiter can incur wake-up and scheduling delays, reducing throughput in a lock convoy','ContextswitchnotalwaysCPUidle.',ja,o=0)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. The designated waiter may not yet be runnable on a CPU, leaving the lock unused even though other threads could acquire it. CPUs can do unrelated work.','Lockidle notCPUidle.',ja,o=0)
f(10,'text','Barging can let a running thread reacquire a cache-hot lock and improve throughput in some workloads','Avoiduniversallyusuallyempirical.',ja,o=1)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. This is one motivation for default-unfair policies, but the workload determines throughput and latency.','UnsupportedmostOSrankingremoved.',ja,o=1)
f(10,'workedExample','Strict hand-off can delay the next critical section until its designated waiter runs. Barging can improve reuse while allowing an unlucky waiter to be repeatedly overtaken. Neither policy alone prevents priority inversion or cycles involving multiple locks. Adaptive policies mitigate starvation, without a universal wall-clock bound. Reducing shared contention can help both policies.','Remove unsupported exactcosts.',ja+go)
